[{"id":"VRCE-6-52-1","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":1,"english":"Hearing the speech of the huge-bodied, longarmed and mighty Kumbhkarma, Mahodara said.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052001","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ce871fccf1ee78d64b21c7e5fafa7973a0392e58993c4753f7b8844d9035f7dd","compact":"988dce50057ef98bb9ce601b5fa27be13a798728b6ff58d26196bdbd27e492b7","nasal":"d820eba1519d65a15d2a0a5cdfd369e32088a27d4456eebb8ae572fd95284e34"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-2","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":2,"english":"O Kumbhakarņa, although sprung in a noble line, you are wondrous haughty and of a vulgar form; and you are not everywhere competent to discern your course.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.818,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052002","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a9158f0c2dc70a68db1084e315457541b6b05630957bdf0b667130225043382b","compact":"15e606ec44c6729f67fa7fae5ac10487237028b224d205d37f4983ba1f6fcc02","nasal":"c8c5056bc3559b30eae14ec6b61953dedbf8bc20dfabd0abc5b119f52e29a020"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-3","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":3,"english":"It certainly cannot well be that the king cannot distinguish between proper and improper; but you has from your youth upwards been insolent; and your joy is in talking perenially.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052003","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5802e6738606f322e545c63b9bbc1438095f21cf43b9dbfe6c040d383827c5ce","compact":"191a2f5e45231318ee46426d1c24a8db54175f91055545d344768e2456ec191e","nasal":"452b4ddadc39ce76ab1ae23d900c03eeb8cc40ecf6291f156384be72d0a5d89f"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-4","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":4,"english":"But the foremost of the Raksasa is versed in place and increase and injury, and know the duties touching season and place, and how to advance his own party and lower that of the foe.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.833,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052004","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"69b14929b52d18ecce4a2113e6e35c3ff9d386b4485d9502262a500d3276cb6c","compact":"6995107a1e91d72becaeeca59ab4db4c7ae2288ee51fd6dd613311528bc62927","nasal":"6995107a1e91d72becaeeca59ab4db4c7ae2288ee51fd6dd613311528bc62927"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-5","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":5,"english":"But what person ever act according to what is proposed feebly by one having strength, yet of inferior intelligence, and who has never respected the aged.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.859,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052005","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"370cc2aa10efc86777a60cd4e081057444512550630e7aeddf1e2e36605ecca3","compact":"94d2b5f8677a1199760fe769de7a05f5d9cbf130ef659cdaef6d28ab9ab9cb0d","nasal":"94d2b5f8677a1199760fe769de7a05f5d9cbf130ef659cdaef6d28ab9ab9cb0d"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-6","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":6,"english":"You are not naturally competent to understand what you yourself says touching the opposed virtue, profit and desire.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.775,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052006","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3ffe32e3063a0f8333690f23793ec6ddda392774e596707323cfad29ed1c74c7","compact":"cbaf3f5a6f0da9a8ea2770ed2ac81c7bbf6b75564cffbd11ffacb4b9d0470532","nasal":"cbaf3f5a6f0da9a8ea2770ed2ac81c7bbf6b75564cffbd11ffacb4b9d0470532"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-7","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":7,"english":"Verily action is the spring of all agencies of happiness or misery; and it is only those that act either well or ill that reap the results of their acts. Virtue, and interest bring about emancipation as well as heaven and prosperity. But evil come from unrighteousness and harms spring therefrom. People reap the fruit of their acts in this world or the next; but the fruit of desire is reaped readily. Therefore should a king set about attaining his desire. And this also had we advised you with our soul. And that is the harm that one should display his prowess before a foe?","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052007","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"01b4e78b0f5493d08fda7551fe308c3bee597e415d3b1d1126fad2610afdf824","compact":"4bdb0ea20557e00bcbfd1b2b78ce61f91a73e6973401a0537914d936cca98863","nasal":"4bdb0ea20557e00bcbfd1b2b78ce61f91a73e6973401a0537914d936cca98863"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-8","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":8,"english":"Verily action is the spring of all agencies of happiness or misery; and it is only those that act either well or ill that reap the results of their acts. Virtue, and interest bring about emancipation as well as heaven and prosperity. But evil come from unrighteousness and harms spring therefrom. People reap the fruit of their acts in this world or the next; but the fruit of desire is reaped readily. Therefore should a king set about attaining his desire. And this also had we advised you with our soul. And that is the harm that one should display his prowess before a foe?","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.625,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052008","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6f43ee8140a586ae23edd2f3d65780721bdf80af8360604a59e8a85a3e289007","compact":"c60e9bc87f3218290cb0389878a8422fe381c942b76707ea9d0b252a2e3c566d","nasal":"c60e9bc87f3218290cb0389878a8422fe381c942b76707ea9d0b252a2e3c566d"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-9","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":9,"english":"Verily action is the spring of all agencies of happiness or misery; and it is only those that act either well or ill that reap the results of their acts. Virtue, and interest bring about emancipation as well as heaven and prosperity. But evil come from unrighteousness and harms spring therefrom. People reap the fruit of their acts in this world or the next; but the fruit of desire is reaped readily. Therefore should a king set about attaining his desire. And this also had we advised you with our soul. And that is the harm that one should display his prowess before a foe?","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.515,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052009","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"78c4326a8570612f34baf6d4e559af3154601d747ea033c6a03934d0c04ed6a7","compact":"650379657ba7123409d4f8db6f87e081bc48d2a4e04ba0484ada850e1007707f","nasal":"cf342f4fab190f86afbda2e157e659ea021768d05fc7ede6625cf88c426cc8a4"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-10","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":10,"english":"Verily action is the spring of all agencies of happiness or misery; and it is only those that act either well or ill that reap the results of their acts. Virtue, and interest bring about emancipation as well as heaven and prosperity. But evil come from unrighteousness and harms spring therefrom. People reap the fruit of their acts in this world or the next; but the fruit of desire is reaped readily. Therefore should a king set about attaining his desire. And this also had we advised you with our soul. And that is the harm that one should display his prowess before a foe?","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.462,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052010","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9052c8308cd726fd7469b5ed04cbb2ee770013ee9a2dafba312396d3a928557e","compact":"0b02b6c0f76399c2671eece3738a37cd4749c689b225a18cc02a33e7beaa3df4","nasal":"0b02b6c0f76399c2671eece3738a37cd4749c689b225a18cc02a33e7beaa3df4"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-11","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":11,"english":"Did you show the reasons for your taking the field alone? But I shall point you out what is improper and unreasonable in this (proposed) course.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.859,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052011","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d4da28521e9d42583b0f9a4827d1340b4cba09feeb13e3e0e0ccc0ae1eb10165","compact":"2dd80ffe3a56e2c125c664863dde0436329aaacba2446756347b3c4014c5bb4d","nasal":"f5e8ab53670985dc47a611da0e93e010ce94f9397fe36d1581330a7d703f7c36"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-12","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":12,"english":"How shall you alone vanquish that Rāghava who formerly routed in Jansthāna innumerable Rākşasas possessed of exceeding strength?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.564,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052012","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e79cbf4e8cdc6dd45e4678da9fb68ad7bd9ef9de9ffd3eddc3d929293cf1948f","compact":"2be37f32f0d74bc1e369ca077feb51320b5eb652325436d35369988e0c217798","nasal":"2be37f32f0d74bc1e369ca077feb51320b5eb652325436d35369988e0c217798"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-13","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":13,"english":"Did you not today in the palace behold those powerful Rākşasas that had been beaten in Janasthana-cowed down in fear?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.779,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052013","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8d725fc83305d63cfc60498b6469964e5666f69ce1d9c4ab4cf7519ffbfa52b5","compact":"b8b67bec7f930963a838c4e1ce770ff42f6dc06b7e4794b58cb3ada160888662","nasal":"b8b67bec7f930963a838c4e1ce770ff42f6dc06b7e4794b58cb3ada160888662"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-14","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":14,"english":"Alas! you wished to awake Rima, son to Daśaratha-knowing full well that le resembles an enraged lion or a sleeping serpeni.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.636,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052014","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"00ec473d4cf50520f85638e21b7ad343d728ae55803a5c951d6bfcbdbf7711ac","compact":"73c4ef2c50a3ed8c57ed63fac49caba64305eb48aa6cc6699072358da9a9f08c","nasal":"73c4ef2c50a3ed8c57ed63fac49caba64305eb48aa6cc6699072358da9a9f08c"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-15","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":15,"english":"But whom does it behove to confront him ever flaming in energy, difficult of being approached when enraged, and unbearable even like Death himself?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052015","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"229fe5fbf6c60abc953169bf559dee445052e6d29065a20303ad0b55243ae733","compact":"2c6128336b9c3fa6323d4c08206cbcc20130c5c6b3acfbf5ffcd91c798e607c3","nasal":"983290ced6923d6a96f5731e43c85bbc73677e734733c86bf038914bc7c5af20"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-16","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":16,"english":"On approaching the foe, this whole host itself shall be imperilled: and therefore, my child, your marching alone does nowise recommend itself to me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.608,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052016","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"888f32561d5fffe25d81761bb72f08553a43c5b7d23bad56289f928784efc7f5","compact":"514401b02a5479963c881496092aa5c1dbea3430ed2504dbd96d60eba236a1bd","nasal":"514401b02a5479963c881496092aa5c1dbea3430ed2504dbd96d60eba236a1bd"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-17","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":17,"english":"Who is there that, albeit weak bent on renouncing his life, wish to bring under subjection a foe that is powerful, as if he would act with reference to one that is inferior?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.761,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052017","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2443081fb751a87cff10392963cca7653cce525bb2729d9d27763142bc2065cf","compact":"c7e1a060124a72049326426a6a9881fff2fdab46ce57e8813fadc4b647c0b7cd","nasal":"c7e1a060124a72049326426a6a9881fff2fdab46ce57e8813fadc4b647c0b7cd"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-18","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":18,"english":"O foremost of Rākasas, why did you wish to fight with him whose peer there is none among men, and who is equal to Indra and Vavasvata himself?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052018","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"95602c5191551a00bd70030e05e83cb33ea4bb271a8704e498ef7e31861d188b","compact":"1a50315e37b70dfc7166ff584571ca951a37f9579e4d2be41d2ef167aa58bd69","nasal":"a39359c52528b9c32ef3de35925369ced566c4316b3770efd970e4b53d2a9f1f"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-19","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":19,"english":"Having said this to Kumbhakarņa wrought up with wrath, Mahodara addressed that destroyer of creature-Ravana-in the midst of the Raksasas.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.724,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052019","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"40c55dd15b781c7a2209dbfdeaa819722cad697835ccaf523957891ebdb57196","compact":"1112cf5ab16cf3ea29be8957dc5bd3c7d20b0b758a0bde712f374f4f638a6268","nasal":"726ec2690dda5f400f790607bd5eda22cf465effd8813c489b70c22f4b119e78"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-20","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":20,"english":"Having already obtained Vaidehi, why did you delay ? If you wish it, Sītā shall come under your control.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.438,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052020","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3237195a5b6c85901fbf515069b73c40f576c599d4f9516bc4d478df8c4854c5","compact":"7e0899adb1259cbd0daad8ea1123e6a1999478220a1b5a9f4ced11c59a541a1e","nasal":"7e0899adb1259cbd0daad8ea1123e6a1999478220a1b5a9f4ced11c59a541a1e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-21","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":21,"english":"I have perceived a way as to how Sītā may smile upon you. Do you listen: and should it please you; act you accordingly.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.735,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052021","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"45fc2e666cc771df1b9cc44db65ebd634efc11eb9628bc7361d1c32d484407d3","compact":"f3bff2ed3dfc5a1be01b9f7afd9becc62798d0ed7d085e2257dcbb5c363a20df","nasal":"f3bff2ed3dfc5a1be01b9f7afd9becc62798d0ed7d085e2257dcbb5c363a20df"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-22","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":22,"english":"Do you proclaim it about that I and Dvijihva and Samhrādī, and Kumbhakarņa and Vitardanathese five-are marching for compassing the destruction of Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.75,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052022","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"edebc0723366a7ffb12034c3ed5e77eb186dbd3b9cde1fbda76c8d8db4346643","compact":"70fcf33eb1182f196ef4299b9bbde7ec0c2cd132e952d44003907a8aae1acb59","nasal":"18e62a1a1f51dae1c0443ceef6081210cae3a69c2c34eaaeee96ae478e631fa3"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-23","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":23,"english":"We, marching out, shall battle with Rāma, putting forth our uttermost. And if we succeed in securing victory, no expedient need we resort to (or bringing Sītā round).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.75,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052023","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"534f90f5f884fd57424604310d4086a6534171b2c8ca3ca8e35c8f3bb8461d92","compact":"0ef91279eabbbbc1e82ae9b825a6b1630d5edbdbceb3bb4c568ed44128811478","nasal":"a4b4acce73d32e458ddacf81b159825b74e441e1b749f21cb7d01b4cab5399d8"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-24","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":24,"english":"But if our foe live after fight and we also having faught the fight, then shall we do what I conceive in my mind.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.947,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052024","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5d2375d172c7dd4e8fcddb1125f20dce0e5a677918164fd64859075f48bb1e7c","compact":"4f6e67eaf54f80866336d217ae290be300a61962c1fd45f92e1d9535ff4b2b3d","nasal":"4f6e67eaf54f80866336d217ae290be300a61962c1fd45f92e1d9535ff4b2b3d"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-25","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":25,"english":"We shall come hither from the field, bathed in blood, and having our bodies cleft with arrows marked with the name of Rama. And we shall give out that both Ráma and Lakşmaņa have been eaten up by us And then we Shall hold your feet, saying, 'Do you gratify us'.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.939,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052025","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d0384ff8054dfed090a499d32c6626e5517141fe7848a84e791eb0d26627369b","compact":"fa834b505a2c862799e2569bd77304dc5fb8718dc5fea07eefacbc29753e92bc","nasal":"e6c9bf3b18faf14859bc0a4ef8b3382dd338d4b57f8dc0985728e4fd0d2614d2"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-26","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":26,"english":"We shall come hither from the field, bathed in blood, and having our bodies cleft with arrows marked with the name of Rama. And we shall give out that both Ráma and Lakşmaņa have been eaten up by us And then we Shall hold your feet, saying, 'Do you gratify us'.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.773,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052026","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7b06de936a86ec2a85cfb1ba7cd7af9a69a73f91d73527451da55292a4c8194e","compact":"bb2cf455fcd8ed3e3203d4a791f05ad985cae4da004e34595c495f4343ce19fb","nasal":"bb2cf455fcd8ed3e3203d4a791f05ad985cae4da004e34595c495f4343ce19fb"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-27","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":27,"english":"Then, O king, do you through Gajaskandha publish it all round that Ráma has been slain along with his brother and the army.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052027","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8d4eecb2b1eb15aab10d0e2d973fc7ac16d19bba9dc1d9deda96a29a4dec202b","compact":"3580cc95a936c3a91ad3bed94dea27bd9833db87ad85c2c500cab0e49c8428e8","nasal":"564468187138ced93a8b867b92b7b0861d4dec20fe7010e833b4053581d7c581"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-52-28","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":52,"verse":28,"english":"O repressor of foes, (pretending to be) well pleased, do you dispense-viands, and servants, and objects of enjoyments, and wealth to your retainers; and to the many heroes and warriors distribute wreaths and attires and unguents. And do you (Simulating) delight drink.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.825,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-64-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6052028","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1e174aab90cf76f16984fc4921d706685f0b4e3833e120f03cf381aa683f5f56","compact":"434ce192260e99a1a4eadd7f625888794d8e29ee49f09ed79136d3d71b1206f0","nasal":"434ce192260e99a1a4eadd7f625888794d8e29ee49f09ed79136d3d71b120